((window.CARDS_BY_LANG = window.CARDS_BY_LANG || {}).en = window.CARDS_BY_LANG.en || {}).invention = [{"name":"Wheel","emoji":"🛞","meta":["about 5,500 years ago","Mesopotamia","mechanics"],"description":"One of humanity's oldest inventions first appeared in Mesopotamia as part of the potter's wheel, and only later found its way onto vehicles. Almost every rotating mechanism descends from it, from farm carts to modern turbines.","fact":"Pre-Columbian peoples of the Americas knew the principle of the wheel — it turns up in children's toys — but never used it for transport.","wiki_en":"Wheel"},{"name":"Writing","emoji":"📜","meta":["about 5,000 years ago","Sumer and Egypt","passing on information"],"description":"A system of signs for recording speech emerged independently in several ancient civilisations — cuneiform in Sumer, hieroglyphs in Egypt. It allowed laws, deals and knowledge to be stored outside of human memory.","fact":"The oldest known texts are not literature but household accounts and lists of goods.","wiki_en":"History of writing"},{"name":"Paper","emoji":"📄","meta":["about AD 100","China","writing and printing"],"description":"A writing material invented in China, where it was made from pounded bark fibres, rags and plants. The secret of its production was guarded for centuries before it spread through the Middle East and on into Europe.","fact":"Legend credits the papermaking technique to a Chinese court official named Cai Lun.","wiki_en":"Papermaking"},{"name":"Gunpowder","emoji":"💥","meta":["around the 9th century","China","warfare"],"description":"A mixture of saltpetre, sulphur and charcoal first obtained by Chinese alchemists who were searching for an elixir of immortality. It went on to power fireworks and then firearms, which changed the way wars were fought.","fact":"Europe took up gunpowder much later than China, with the first cannon appearing in the 14th century.","wiki_en":"Gunpowder"},{"name":"Compass","emoji":"🧭","meta":["around the 11th century","China","navigation"],"description":"A device that relies on a magnetised needle turning towards Earth's magnetic poles, first described in China. It became the seafarer's most important instrument, making long voyages possible with no landmarks in sight.","fact":"The earliest Chinese compasses were used not for sailing but for divination and feng shui geomancy.","wiki_en":"Compass"},{"name":"Printing press","emoji":"🖨️","meta":["about 1440","Johannes Gutenberg","book printing"],"description":"A press with movable type for turning out text quickly, built by the German craftsman Johannes Gutenberg. It made books far cheaper to produce and noticeably sped up the spread of knowledge across Europe.","fact":"The first major book Gutenberg printed was the Bible, known ever since as the Gutenberg Bible.","wiki_en":"Printing press"},{"name":"Glasses","emoji":"👓","meta":["late 13th century","Italy","vision correction"],"description":"Lenses to improve eyesight appeared in medieval Italy, probably in Pisa or Venice, where craftsmen learned to grind glass to the right shape. Over time spectacles became an affordable way of coping with age-related farsightedness.","fact":"The first spectacles had no arms to hook over the ears — you held them in your hand or pinched them onto your nose.","wiki_en":"Glasses"},{"name":"Telescope","emoji":"🔭","meta":["1608","the Netherlands","astronomy"],"description":"An optical instrument for viewing distant objects, on record since 1608, when the Dutch craftsman Hans Lippershey filed a patent application for it. Galileo Galilei later improved the design, pointed it at the sky and found the moons of Jupiter.","fact":"Galileo did not invent the telescope, but he was the first to use it systematically for astronomical observation.","wiki_en":"Telescope"},{"name":"Microscope","emoji":"🔬","meta":["late 16th century","the Netherlands","optics"],"description":"An instrument for magnifying tiny objects, built by Dutch spectacle makers — the Janssen family among them. Antonie van Leeuwenhoek later used one to become the first person ever to see bacteria and other micro-organisms.","fact":"Leeuwenhoek reached enormous magnifications with a single tiny lens that he ground himself.","wiki_en":"Microscope"},{"name":"Mechanical clock","emoji":"🕰️","meta":["around the 13th century","Western Europe","measuring time"],"description":"Devices with toothed wheels and an escapement to count out time evenly appeared in medieval European monasteries. They replaced water clocks and hourglasses and let towns organise their day far more precisely.","fact":"The first mechanical clocks were mounted in cathedral towers and had no dial — they only struck the hours with a bell.","wiki_en":"History of timekeeping devices"},{"name":"Steam engine","emoji":"⚙️","meta":["1769","James Watt","industry"],"description":"An engine that turns the energy of steam into mechanical work, much improved by the Scottish engineer James Watt, who added a separate condenser to earlier designs. The invention became the driving force of the Industrial Revolution.","fact":"Thomas Newcomen had built working steam engines before Watt, but they burned fuel appallingly inefficiently.","wiki_en":"Steam engine"},{"name":"Steam locomotive","emoji":"🚂","meta":["1804","Richard Trevithick","rail transport"],"description":"The first working locomotive driven by steam was built by the British inventor Richard Trevithick. George Stephenson later refined the design and built the first railways open to the general public.","fact":"Trevithick's locomotive made its debut at an ironworks, hauling a load of iron and a crowd of people along the factory rails.","wiki_en":"Steam locomotive"},{"name":"Steamboat","emoji":"🚢","meta":["late 18th century","several inventors","water transport"],"description":"Steam-powered vessels were tried in several countries, but the first commercial success was the boat of the American Robert Fulton, which began scheduled runs on the Hudson River. Steamers went on to push sail aside on rivers and oceans alike.","fact":"Fulton's steamboat was nicknamed \"Fulton's Folly\" by contemporaries who expected the venture to fail.","wiki_en":"Steamboat"},{"name":"Incandescent light bulb","emoji":"💡","meta":["19th century","pioneers included Edison and Lodygin","lighting"],"description":"Several inventors in different countries worked on a practical electric lamp, among them Alexander Lodygin and Joseph Swan. Thomas Edison brought the design to commercial success by finding a filament that lasted.","fact":"Edison tried thousands of materials for the filament before settling on carbonised bamboo fibre.","wiki_en":"Incandescent light bulb"},{"name":"Electric battery","emoji":"🔋","meta":["1800","Alessandro Volta","sources of current"],"description":"The first source of steady electric current — the voltaic pile, a stack of alternating zinc and copper discs separated by cloth soaked in brine — was built by the Italian physicist Alessandro Volta. It opened the age of practical electricity.","fact":"The unit of electric voltage, the volt, is named after Volta.","wiki_en":"Electric battery"},{"name":"Telegraph","emoji":"📡","meta":["1830s","Samuel Morse and others","communications"],"description":"The electric telegraph, which sent messages along wires, was developed independently by several inventors, but the best known system was Samuel Morse's, with its alphabet of dots and dashes. For the first time news could cross vast distances almost instantly.","fact":"Morse did not devise Morse code alone — his assistant Alfred Vail contributed a great deal to it.","wiki_en":"Electrical telegraph"},{"name":"Telephone","emoji":"☎️","meta":["1876","patented by Alexander Bell","communications"],"description":"A device for carrying the human voice along wires, patented by Alexander Graham Bell, though others — Elisha Gray among them — were working on much the same thing at the same time. The telephone quickly reshaped business and everyday life.","fact":"Bell's application and his rival Elisha Gray's papers reached the patent office on the very same day: 14 February 1876.","wiki_en":"Telephone"},{"name":"Radio","emoji":"📻","meta":["late 19th century","pioneers included Popov and Marconi","communications"],"description":"Sending signals without wires, on electromagnetic waves, was demonstrated independently by several inventors, including Alexander Popov and Guglielmo Marconi. Marconi was the first to make the technology commercial and to open a transatlantic link.","fact":"Russia marks Radio Day on 7 May, the date Popov publicly demonstrated his receiver.","wiki_en":"Radio"},{"name":"Photography","emoji":"📷","meta":["1820s","Nicéphore Niépce","images"],"description":"The first lasting picture taken from life was made by the Frenchman Joseph Nicéphore Niépce, using a camera obscura and a light-sensitive coating. Louis Daguerre later refined the process into the sharper and faster daguerreotype.","fact":"Niépce's earliest surviving photograph needed an exposure of several hours.","wiki_en":"Photography"},{"name":"Cinema","emoji":"🎬","meta":["1895","the Lumière brothers","moving pictures"],"description":"The first public paid film screening with a projector was staged by the French brothers Auguste and Louis Lumière in a Paris café. Other inventors had run similar experiments before them, including Thomas Edison with his kinetoscope.","fact":"One of the Lumières' earliest films, \"The Arrival of a Train\", supposedly made viewers leap out of their seats in fright.","wiki_en":"Cinematograph"},{"name":"Television","emoji":"📺","meta":["1920s–1930s","pioneers included Zworykin and Baird","broadcasting"],"description":"Sending moving pictures over a distance took the work of several inventors: the Scot John Logie Baird showed a mechanical system, while Vladimir Zworykin and Philo Farnsworth developed the electronic technology that modern TV is built on.","fact":"Baird's first broadcasts relied on a spinning disc rather than electronic scanning.","wiki_en":"History of television"},{"name":"Phonograph","emoji":"🎙️","meta":["1877","Thomas Edison","sound recording"],"description":"The first machine to record and play back sound, writing it onto tinfoil, was built by the American inventor Thomas Edison. The phonograph set the whole recording industry in motion, though the handier gramophone later displaced it.","fact":"The first words Edison recorded on his phonograph were the nursery rhyme about Mary and her little lamb.","wiki_en":"Phonograph"},{"name":"Internal combustion engine","emoji":"🔧","meta":["19th century","several inventors","mechanics"],"description":"An engine that burns its fuel inside the working cylinder was pursued by many engineers, and the German Nikolaus Otto produced the practical four-stroke version. Karl Benz and Gottlieb Daimler soon put such engines into the first cars.","fact":"The earliest engine of this kind ran on coal gas rather than petrol.","wiki_en":"Internal combustion engine"},{"name":"Car","emoji":"🚗","meta":["1886","Karl Benz","transport"],"description":"The first practical automobile with a petrol engine was patented by the German engineer Karl Benz. His wife Bertha Benz then drove it on the first long-distance road trip in history, proving that the invention really worked.","fact":"Bertha Benz bought fuel for her journey at a pharmacy — at the time there was simply nowhere else that sold it.","wiki_en":"Car"},{"name":"Bicycle","emoji":"🚲","meta":["early 19th century","Karl von Drais","transport"],"description":"The bicycle's ancestor — a wooden running machine with no pedals — was dreamt up by the German baron Karl von Drais. Other inventors added pedals and a chain drive later, turning the draisine into the two-wheeler we know today.","fact":"The first bicycles had no pedals: the rider pushed off the ground with both feet, as on a scooter.","wiki_en":"Bicycle"},{"name":"Motorcycle","emoji":"🏍️","meta":["1885","Gottlieb Daimler","transport"],"description":"The first two-wheeler powered by an internal combustion engine was built by the Germans Gottlieb Daimler and Wilhelm Maybach. Its wooden frame carried small extra wheels on either side to keep the machine upright.","fact":"Daimler's first motorcycle was called a \"riding car\" — the word motorcycle itself came along later.","wiki_en":"Motorcycle"},{"name":"Airplane","emoji":"✈️","meta":["1903","pioneers included the Wright brothers","aviation"],"description":"The first controlled flight of a powered machine heavier than air was made by the Americans Wilbur and Orville Wright at Kitty Hawk. Decades of experiments by glider pilots and engineers around the world led up to it.","fact":"The Wright brothers' first flight lasted about twelve seconds and covered less than forty metres.","wiki_en":"Airplane"},{"name":"Helicopter","emoji":"🚁","meta":["1939","Igor Sikorsky","aviation"],"description":"The first practical helicopter, with a single main rotor and a tail rotor, was built by the Russian-born American engineer Igor Sikorsky. Many inventors had explored the idea of a rotary-wing machine before him, Leonardo da Vinci among them in his sketches.","fact":"Sikorsky left Russia after the revolution and went on with his career as a designer in the United States.","wiki_en":"Helicopter"},{"name":"Hot air balloon","emoji":"🎈","meta":["1783","the Montgolfier brothers","aeronautics"],"description":"The first manned flight in a hot air balloon was arranged by the French brothers Joseph and Étienne Montgolfier. Their first ascent with passengers over Paris was a sensation and the start of the age of flight.","fact":"Before sending people up, the Montgolfiers gave the very first ride to a sheep, a duck and a rooster.","wiki_en":"Hot air balloon"},{"name":"Airship","emoji":"🛸","meta":["1900","Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin","aeronautics"],"description":"A steerable aircraft built around a rigid frame, created by the German designer Count Ferdinand von Zeppelin, whose name became the common word for such craft. Airships carried passengers for only a few decades before aeroplanes pushed them out.","fact":"The Hindenburg disaster of 1937 largely brought the era of passenger airships to an end.","wiki_en":"Airship"},{"name":"Parachute","emoji":"🪂","meta":["late 18th century","Louis-Sébastien Lenormand","flight safety"],"description":"The first successful jump with a parachute of his own design was made by the Frenchman Louis-Sébastien Lenormand, who leapt from the observatory tower in Montpellier. Later designs added a pack and a canopy of stronger fabric.","fact":"Lenormand coined the word parachute himself, joining the Greek para with the French chute, meaning a fall.","wiki_en":"Parachute"},{"name":"Rocket","emoji":"🚀","meta":["13th century","China","warfare and spaceflight"],"description":"Simple gunpowder rockets for fireworks and for war appeared in medieval China. In the twentieth century pioneers of rocketry such as Konstantin Tsiolkovsky and Robert Goddard developed the idea, which led to rockets able to reach space.","fact":"China was already using rockets in battle against Mongol troops in the 13th century.","wiki_en":"Rocket"},{"name":"Artificial satellite","emoji":"🛰️","meta":["1957","the USSR","spaceflight"],"description":"The first artificial satellite of the Earth — Sputnik 1, a simple metal sphere with antennas — was launched by the Soviet Union. Its signal, which radio amateurs everywhere could pick up, opened the space age and set off the race between the space powers.","fact":"Sputnik 1 broadcast a simple beep-beep that radio stations right across the planet could hear.","wiki_en":"Sputnik 1"},{"name":"Submarine","emoji":"🛥️","meta":["early 17th century","Cornelis Drebbel","navy"],"description":"The first working underwater craft that could dive and move beneath the surface was built by the Dutch inventor Cornelis Drebbel for the English court. Submarines later became one of the most important parts of any navy, above all in the 20th century.","fact":"Drebbel's first submarine was propelled by ordinary oars, pushed out through leather seals.","wiki_en":"Submarine"},{"name":"Dynamite","emoji":"🧨","meta":["1867","Alfred Nobel","explosives"],"description":"A safe-to-handle explosive made by soaking nitroglycerine into a porous material was invented by the Swedish chemist Alfred Nobel. The fortune he made from dynamite later paid for the famous Nobel Prize.","fact":"Nobel founded his prize partly because he did not want history to remember him only as a \"merchant of death\".","wiki_en":"Dynamite"},{"name":"Atomic bomb","emoji":"☢️","meta":["1945","the Manhattan Project, USA","warfare"],"description":"The first nuclear weapon was built by a team of scientists and engineers on the Manhattan Project, led by the physicist Robert Oppenheimer. Its first use in war, over Japan in 1945, changed thinking about war and international security for good.","fact":"The first test of an atomic bomb was code-named Trinity and took place in the New Mexico desert.","wiki_en":"Nuclear weapon"},{"name":"Nuclear power plant","emoji":"⚛️","meta":["1954","the USSR","energy"],"description":"The world's first nuclear power station connected to an electricity grid was built in the Soviet town of Obninsk. It proved that nuclear energy could be put to peaceful use and made to generate electricity.","fact":"The Obninsk plant ran for almost half a century and was shut down only in 2002.","wiki_en":"Nuclear power plant"},{"name":"Kalashnikov rifle","emoji":"🔫","meta":["1947","Mikhail Kalashnikov","small arms"],"description":"An automatic carbine chambered for an intermediate cartridge, designed by the Soviet engineer Mikhail Kalashnikov. Simple and reliable, the AK became one of the most widespread small arms anywhere in the world.","fact":"The 47 in AK-47 points to the year the design itself is dated to: 1947.","wiki_en":"AK-47"},{"name":"Radar","emoji":"📡","meta":["1930s","several countries independently","warfare"],"description":"Technology for spotting objects by the radio waves they reflect was developed independently in several countries on the eve of the Second World War, Britain and Germany among them. Radar played a decisive part in Britain's defence during the Battle of Britain.","fact":"Radar started life as an acronym for \"radio detection and ranging\".","wiki_en":"Radar"},{"name":"Laser","emoji":"🔴","meta":["1960","Theodore Maiman","optics"],"description":"The first working laser, built around a ruby crystal, was assembled by the American physicist Theodore Maiman. It rested on the theoretical work of many scientists and quickly found uses in medicine, communications and industry.","fact":"Laser is an acronym: light amplification by stimulated emission of radiation.","wiki_en":"Laser"},{"name":"Transistor","emoji":"⚡","meta":["1947","Bardeen, Brattain and Shockley","electronics"],"description":"The semiconductor device that replaced bulky vacuum tubes was created at Bell Labs in the United States by the physicists John Bardeen, Walter Brattain and William Shockley. The transistor underlies all modern electronics, from radio sets to computers.","fact":"All three of the transistor's creators shared the Nobel Prize in Physics for the invention.","wiki_en":"Transistor"},{"name":"Microprocessor","emoji":"🧮","meta":["1971","Intel","computing"],"description":"The first mass-produced microprocessor, the Intel 4004, packed the whole logic of a processor onto a single chip and was released by the American company Intel. The invention is what made compact personal computers possible.","fact":"The first Intel microprocessor was designed as a chip for a Japanese calculator, not for a computer.","wiki_en":"Microprocessor"},{"name":"Computer","emoji":"🖥️","meta":["mid-20th century","several inventors","computing"],"description":"Electronic computing machines appeared in the 1940s through the work of different engineering teams in Britain, the United States and Germany — ENIAC and Colossus are among the early examples. Computers slowly shrank from vast valve-driven machines into devices anyone can own.","fact":"ENIAC, one of the first electronic computers, weighed around 27 tonnes and filled an entire room.","wiki_en":"Computer"},{"name":"Personal computer","emoji":"💻","meta":["1970s","several companies","computing"],"description":"Compact, affordable computers for the home and the office went on sale in the 1970s from several companies, including Apple with the Apple II and IBM with the IBM PC. The personal computer turned computing into something everyone could have.","fact":"Early personal computers were sold as kits for enthusiasts to put together themselves.","wiki_en":"Personal computer"},{"name":"Computer mouse","emoji":"🖱️","meta":["1964","Douglas Engelbart","input devices"],"description":"The first prototype of a pointing device for moving a cursor around a screen was built by the American engineer Douglas Engelbart. It got its name from the cable trailing out of the back, which looked like a mouse's tail.","fact":"Engelbart's first mouse was made of wood and had just one button.","wiki_en":"Computer mouse"},{"name":"Internet","emoji":"🌐","meta":["1960s–1970s","the ARPANET project, USA","communications"],"description":"A packet-switched network linking computers at different institutions was started by American researchers under ARPANET, a military and academic project. In time the technology grew into the global network that connects the whole world.","fact":"The first message sent over the internet's predecessor network broke off after the first two letters because of a crash.","wiki_en":"Internet"},{"name":"World Wide Web","emoji":"🕸️","meta":["1989","Tim Berners-Lee","communications"],"description":"A system of hypertext pages joined by links and read through a browser, thought up by the British scientist Tim Berners-Lee while he was working at CERN. His invention turned the internet from a technical network into a space anyone could use.","fact":"In 1993 CERN gave up its rights to the web and put the technology into the public domain, free for everyone.","wiki_en":"World Wide Web"},{"name":"Email","emoji":"📧","meta":["1971","Ray Tomlinson","communications"],"description":"A way of passing messages between different computers on a network was worked out by the American programmer Ray Tomlinson. He was also the one who proposed the @ sign to separate a user's name from the name of the destination machine.","fact":"Tomlinson himself could not remember what his first test message said — something forgettable, by his own account.","wiki_en":"Email"},{"name":"Mobile phone","emoji":"📱","meta":["1973","Martin Cooper","communications"],"description":"The first call from a portable wireless telephone was made by Martin Cooper, an engineer at Motorola, who rang a rival research lab while standing in the street. It took another decade or so before such devices went on sale to the public.","fact":"The first mobile phone weighed about a kilogram and its battery lasted only half an hour.","wiki_en":"Mobile phone"},{"name":"Smartphone","emoji":"📲","meta":["1992–1994","IBM Simon","communications and computing"],"description":"The IBM Simon is considered the first device to combine a telephone with computer functions and a touchscreen. The category only went mainstream much later, when Apple's iPhone set the shape of the modern smartphone.","fact":"The IBM Simon could already send faxes and email in the early 1990s.","wiki_en":"Smartphone"},{"name":"GPS","emoji":"🛰️","meta":["1970s–1990s","the US Department of Defense","navigation"],"description":"A system for fixing your position anywhere on Earth from satellite signals was developed by the American military and its engineers for army use, and later opened up to civilians. It now underpins navigation in cars, phones and aircraft.","fact":"The full constellation of more than two dozen GPS satellites was only completed in the mid-1990s.","wiki_en":"Global Positioning System"},{"name":"Wi-Fi","emoji":"📶","meta":["1997","developed collectively as a standard","wireless communications"],"description":"The standard for sending data wirelessly between devices was worked out by a group of engineers drafting the IEEE 802.11 specification. The name Wi-Fi was invented later by marketers as an easier label for the technology.","fact":"Wi-Fi is not an abbreviation at all, although it is often wrongly expanded as \"wireless fidelity\".","wiki_en":"Wi-Fi"},{"name":"Bluetooth","emoji":"🔵","meta":["1994","Ericsson","wireless communications"],"description":"Short-range wireless technology was developed by engineers at the Swedish company Ericsson. The name and the logo both nod to the Scandinavian king Harald Bluetooth, who united the Danish lands under one crown.","fact":"The Bluetooth logo is made of two runes, the initials of King Harald Bluetooth.","wiki_en":"Bluetooth"},{"name":"USB flash drive","emoji":"💾","meta":["late 1990s","several companies","data storage"],"description":"Compact flash-memory drives with a USB plug were offered almost simultaneously by several companies, the Israeli firm M-Systems among them. They quickly pushed floppy disks aside as the handiest way of carrying files between computers.","fact":"The first USB sticks held only a few dozen megabytes — nowhere near enough for a single high-quality film.","wiki_en":"USB flash drive"},{"name":"Compact disc","emoji":"💿","meta":["1982","Sony and Philips","data storage and sound"],"description":"An optical disc for recording sound digitally, developed jointly by the companies Sony and Philips. For decades the CD was the main medium for music, pushing aside both vinyl records and cassette tapes.","fact":"The first CDs held 74 minutes — a length demanded, so the story goes, by one engineer's favourite symphony.","wiki_en":"Compact disc"},{"name":"Digital camera","emoji":"📷","meta":["1975","Steven Sasson, Kodak","photography"],"description":"The first working prototype of a digital camera was put together by Steven Sasson, an engineer at Kodak. It recorded a black-and-white image onto a cassette and was the size of a toaster, but it set the principle of all digital photography since.","fact":"Sasson's first digital image took about 23 seconds to write onto the tape in the cassette.","wiki_en":"Digital camera"},{"name":"3D printer","emoji":"🖨️","meta":["1980s","Chuck Hull","manufacturing"],"description":"A technique for building three-dimensional objects layer by layer out of a liquid polymer hardened by a laser was patented by the American engineer Chuck Hull. He also coined the term stereolithography, one of the first forms of 3D printing.","fact":"3D Systems, the company Chuck Hull founded after the invention, is still a major player in 3D printing.","wiki_en":"3D printing"},{"name":"Drone","emoji":"🛩️","meta":["20th century","military projects in various countries","aviation"],"description":"Aircraft with no pilot on board were developed throughout the 20th century by military engineers in many countries, from radio-controlled targets to modern reconnaissance drones. Today they are used by armies, by film crews and for delivering goods.","fact":"Some of the earliest unmanned aircraft served as flying targets for anti-aircraft gunners to practise on.","wiki_en":"Unmanned aerial vehicle"},{"name":"Electric car","emoji":"🔌","meta":["19th century","several inventors","transport"],"description":"The first carriages with an electric motor appeared as early as the 19th century, thanks to several independent inventors in Europe and America, long before petrol cars. Their limited range meant they lost out to the internal combustion engine for a century.","fact":"In the early 1900s electric cars were very popular with women drivers: there was no heavy crank to start them.","wiki_en":"Electric vehicle"},{"name":"Solar cell","emoji":"☀️","meta":["1954","Bell Labs","energy"],"description":"The first practical silicon solar cell, able to turn sunlight into electricity efficiently, was built by engineers at the American company Bell Labs. The invention opened the way to solar power, from satellites to the roofs of ordinary houses.","fact":"Among the first users of solar cells were space satellites, where almost no other power source will do.","wiki_en":"Solar cell"},{"name":"Wind turbine","emoji":"💨","meta":["late 19th century","James Blyth","energy"],"description":"One of the first windmills built to generate electricity was put up by the Scottish engineer James Blyth to light his own house. From that idea modern wind turbines have grown into huge industrial machines that power whole regions.","fact":"Blyth's neighbours at first turned down the free electricity he offered them, calling it the work of the devil.","wiki_en":"Wind turbine"},{"name":"Light-emitting diode","emoji":"💡","meta":["1962","Nick Holonyak","lighting and electronics"],"description":"The first LED to glow in visible red light was made by the American engineer Nick Holonyak, then working at General Electric. Diodes in other colours, blue above all, later made energy-efficient lamps and screens possible.","fact":"The blue LED, which completed the palette of colours, won its inventors the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2014.","wiki_en":"Light-emitting diode"},{"name":"Refrigerator","emoji":"🧊","meta":["19th century","several inventors","household appliances"],"description":"Machines for cooling food artificially were developed by various engineers through the 19th century, and the fridge became a mass-market household appliance in the early 20th thanks to firms like General Electric. It freed people from buying ice to keep food fresh.","fact":"The first domestic refrigerators were so expensive that they cost as much as a small car.","wiki_en":"Refrigerator"},{"name":"Washing machine","emoji":"🧺","meta":["19th–20th centuries","several inventors","household appliances"],"description":"Mechanical devices for washing laundry were refined by engineers throughout the 19th century, and the American Alva Fisher was among the first to sell an electric washing machine in the early 20th. The invention made housework, most of it women's, far lighter.","fact":"Early electric washing machines had no safety guards, and their wringer rollers often injured the hands of the women using them.","wiki_en":"Washing machine"},{"name":"Microwave oven","emoji":"📦","meta":["1945","Percy Spencer","household appliances"],"description":"The American engineer Percy Spencer stumbled on the fact that microwaves heat food when he noticed a chocolate bar melting in his pocket beside a working magnetron. That accident led to the idea of a domestic oven that cooks a meal in minutes.","fact":"The first commercial microwave oven was the size of a refrigerator and weighed more than 300 kilograms.","wiki_en":"Microwave oven"},{"name":"Vacuum cleaner","emoji":"🧹","meta":["early 20th century","Hubert Cecil Booth","household appliances"],"description":"The first effective vacuum cleaner, which sucked air in rather than blowing dust about, was built by the British engineer Hubert Cecil Booth. Early machines were so bulky that they were hauled through the streets on a horse cart, with the hose fed in through the windows.","fact":"Booth tested his idea in a London restaurant, pressing a handkerchief-covered tube to a chair and finding the dust caught in the cloth.","wiki_en":"Vacuum cleaner"},{"name":"Air conditioning","emoji":"❄️","meta":["1902","Willis Carrier","climate control"],"description":"The first system for controlling the temperature and humidity of air was built by the American engineer Willis Carrier for a printing house, where damp air was ruining the print. Air conditioning later spread to homes, offices and cars all over the world.","fact":"The company Carrier founded is still one of the largest makers of climate control equipment.","wiki_en":"Air conditioning"},{"name":"Sewing machine","emoji":"🧵","meta":["19th century","several inventors","clothing manufacture"],"description":"Several inventors worked on a mechanism to automate sewing: the American Elias Howe patented a machine with a lockstitch, and Isaac Singer then turned the idea into a commercial success. The sewing machine sharply sped up the making of clothes.","fact":"Howe and Singer fought a long series of court battles over the rights to the sewing machine.","wiki_en":"Sewing machine"},{"name":"Elevator","emoji":"🛗","meta":["1852","Elisha Otis","construction"],"description":"A safe passenger lift with a mechanism that stops the car from falling if the cable snaps was invented by the American Elisha Otis. It was that safety device, more than the lift itself, that made tall buildings and skyscrapers possible.","fact":"Otis demonstrated his lift's safety in public by ordering the cable cut while he stood in the car.","wiki_en":"Elevator"},{"name":"Escalator","emoji":"↕️","meta":["late 19th century","several inventors","moving people"],"description":"A moving sloped staircase for carrying people upwards was proposed independently by several American inventors, and the trade name escalator was registered by the Otis Elevator Company. The device became a fixture of shops, stations and the underground.","fact":"Escalator was originally a registered trademark and only later turned into an ordinary word.","wiki_en":"Escalator"},{"name":"Zipper","emoji":"🤐","meta":["1893–1913","Judson and Sundback","clothing"],"description":"Early versions of a fastener based on interlocking teeth were proposed by the American Whitcomb Judson, and the convenient modern design was perfected by the Swedish-American engineer Gideon Sundback. The zip gradually replaced buttons and laces on clothes and shoes.","fact":"The first zips were used in bulk not on clothing but on boots and tobacco pouches.","wiki_en":"Zipper"},{"name":"Velcro","emoji":"🪢","meta":["1941","George de Mestral","materials"],"description":"The Swiss engineer George de Mestral took the idea of a fastener made of two fabric strips, one of hooks and one of loops, from nature: under the microscope he studied the burrs clinging to his clothes and to his dog's coat. The result is sold as Velcro.","fact":"The name Velcro is made of the French words for velvet and hook.","wiki_en":"Hook-and-loop fastener"},{"name":"Jeans","emoji":"👖","meta":["1873","Strauss and Davis","clothing"],"description":"Tough work trousers of heavy cotton cloth with metal rivets at the pockets were patented by the American merchant Levi Strauss and the tailor Jacob Davis. First worn by gold prospectors and labourers, jeans became one of the symbols of 20th-century mass fashion.","fact":"The famous rivets on the back pockets were put there so the pockets would not tear under a prospector's tools.","wiki_en":"Jeans"},{"name":"Ballpoint pen","emoji":"🖊️","meta":["1938","László Bíró","writing"],"description":"A pen with a rotating ball at the tip that fed quick-drying ink evenly onto the paper was patented by the Hungarian journalist László Bíró. His surname is still used in many countries as the everyday word for a ballpoint pen.","fact":"In Britain and elsewhere a ballpoint pen is still often called simply a biro, after its inventor.","wiki_en":"Ballpoint pen"},{"name":"Pencil","emoji":"✏️","meta":["16th century","England","writing and drawing"],"description":"The modern pencil with a graphite core appeared in England after a large deposit of pure graphite was found in Cumberland. The French engineer Nicolas-Jacques Conté later worked out how to mix graphite with clay, which made the hardness of the lead adjustable.","fact":"Graphite was long mistaken for a kind of lead, which is why a pencil's core is called its lead to this day.","wiki_en":"Pencil"},{"name":"Adhesive tape","emoji":"🎗️","meta":["1930","Richard Drew","materials and packaging"],"description":"Transparent adhesive tape on a cellophane backing was invented by Richard Drew, an engineer at the American company 3M. He had already come up with masking tape, and his clear tape quickly became indispensable at home and in the office.","fact":"The name Scotch tape comes from a story that the glue on the first samples was spread so sparingly it seemed stingy, or \"Scotch\".","wiki_en":"Adhesive tape"},{"name":"Plastic","emoji":"🧴","meta":["1907","Leo Baekeland","materials"],"description":"The first fully synthetic plastic — Bakelite, a material with no equivalent in nature — was created by the Belgian-born American chemist Leo Baekeland. The invention opened the age of mass-produced plastic goods for every purpose imaginable.","fact":"Bakelite was used for all sorts of things, from telephone and radio housings to door handles and kitchen utensils.","wiki_en":"Bakelite"},{"name":"Concrete","emoji":"🧱","meta":["ancient Rome","Roman engineers","construction"],"description":"A building material of binder mixed with aggregate was already in heavy use by Roman engineers, who added volcanic ash to the mix for strength. Modern Portland cement, the basis of today's concrete, was patented in the 19th century by the Briton Joseph Aspdin.","fact":"The dome of the Roman Pantheon, cast in concrete nearly two thousand years ago, is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world.","wiki_en":"Concrete"},{"name":"Glass","emoji":"🪟","meta":["about 3500 BC","Mesopotamia and Egypt","materials"],"description":"Making glass from melted sand with additives was mastered by craftsmen in ancient Mesopotamia and Egypt. The blowing technique, invented later in the Middle East, made thin vessels possible and, in the end, window panes as well.","fact":"The oldest glass objects were not windows or tableware but imitation gemstones set into jewellery.","wiki_en":"Glass"},{"name":"Mirror","emoji":"🪞","meta":["antiquity, improved in the 16th century","Venice","everyday life"],"description":"Polished mirrors of metal and obsidian were used in antiquity, but the first good glass mirrors, backed with an amalgam of tin and mercury, were made in quantity by Venetian craftsmen on the island of Murano. Their method was kept secret for centuries.","fact":"Venice guarded the secret of mirror-making so jealously that craftsmen who left the island faced harsh punishment.","wiki_en":"Mirror"},{"name":"Soap","emoji":"🧼","meta":["antiquity","Mesopotamia","hygiene"],"description":"Simple washing mixtures of fat and ash were used in ancient Mesopotamia and Babylon, as surviving clay tablets with recipes show. Hard soap in the form we know spread much later, reaching medieval Europe through the Arab world.","fact":"The oldest known soap recipe is written on a Babylonian clay tablet some four thousand years old.","wiki_en":"Soap"},{"name":"Toothbrush","emoji":"🪥","meta":["15th century","China","hygiene"],"description":"Brushes for cleaning teeth, with bristles set into a bone or bamboo handle, were first made in China from the stiff hair of a boar's back. Similar brushes reached Europe centuries later, and mass production only arrived in the 19th and 20th centuries.","fact":"Synthetic nylon bristles only appeared on brushes in the late 1930s; before that they came from animals.","wiki_en":"Toothbrush"},{"name":"Flush toilet","emoji":"🚽","meta":["16th–19th centuries","Harington and Crapper","plumbing"],"description":"One of the first flushing lavatories was built by the court poet John Harington back in the 16th century, but it never caught on. The modern toilet with a water seal was refined and spread by 19th-century engineers, Thomas Crapper among them — and he is often wrongly named as its inventor.","fact":"Contrary to the popular story, Crapper's surname is not where the English slang word for a toilet comes from.","wiki_en":"Flush toilet"},{"name":"Canned food","emoji":"🥫","meta":["1810","Appert and Durand","food preservation"],"description":"A way of keeping food for a long time in sealed jars after heating was devised by the French cook Nicolas Appert on a commission from Napoleon's army. The Briton Peter Durand soon patented a version using a metal tin instead of glass.","fact":"The tin opener appeared only decades after the tin itself — at first cans were opened with a hammer and chisel.","wiki_en":"Canning"},{"name":"Pasteurisation","emoji":"🥛","meta":["1864","Louis Pasteur","food industry"],"description":"A method of heating liquid foods to kill harmful micro-organisms without spoiling the taste was worked out by the French scientist Louis Pasteur, who was studying why wine and beer turn sour. It was later applied widely to milk, making it safer to keep and to drink.","fact":"Pasteur developed the method first of all to save the French wine industry, not for milk.","wiki_en":"Pasteurization"},{"name":"Vaccine","emoji":"💉","meta":["1796","Edward Jenner","medicine"],"description":"The first scientifically grounded inoculation against smallpox was created by the British doctor Edward Jenner, who noticed that milkmaids who had had cowpox did not catch the human disease. His method began all modern vaccination, which has saved millions of lives.","fact":"The word vaccine comes from the Latin for cow, since the first material for inoculation was taken from one.","wiki_en":"Vaccine"},{"name":"Penicillin","emoji":"🦠","meta":["1928","Alexander Fleming","medicine"],"description":"The first widely used antibiotic was found by accident by the British bacteriologist Alexander Fleming, who noticed that mould on a forgotten dish of bacteria was holding back their growth. Other scientists later made it fit for mass production, and it went on to save countless lives during the Second World War and after it.","fact":"Fleming almost threw the mouldy Petri dish away before he noticed the odd clear ring around the mould.","wiki_en":"Penicillin"},{"name":"General anaesthesia","emoji":"💊","meta":["1846","William Morton","medicine"],"description":"The first public demonstration of general anaesthesia using ether was given by the American dentist William Morton at a hospital in Boston. It opened the era of painless surgery; until then operations were an ordeal endured with nothing to dull the pain.","fact":"Before anaesthesia, surgeons were prized not only for skill but for speed, since the patient bore the pain fully conscious.","wiki_en":"General anaesthesia"},{"name":"X-ray machine","emoji":"🩻","meta":["1895","Wilhelm Röntgen","medicine"],"description":"Radiation able to pass through dense tissue and show the bones beneath the skin was discovered by chance by the German physicist Wilhelm Röntgen during experiments with a cathode ray tube. The discovery won him the first Nobel Prize in Physics ever awarded.","fact":"The first X-ray image in history was one Röntgen made of his own wife's hand.","wiki_en":"X-ray"},{"name":"MRI","emoji":"🧲","meta":["1970s","Lauterbur and Mansfield","medicine"],"description":"A way of imaging the inside of the body with a magnetic field and no harmful radiation was developed by the American chemist Paul Lauterbur and the British physicist Peter Mansfield. Magnetic resonance imaging became one of the key diagnostic tools of modern medicine.","fact":"The creators of MRI received the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for it only in the 21st century.","wiki_en":"Magnetic resonance imaging"},{"name":"Pacemaker","emoji":"❤️","meta":["1958","Elmqvist and Senning","medicine"],"description":"The first implantable cardiac pacemaker, placed inside a patient's body, was designed and fitted by the Swedish engineer Rune Elmqvist and the surgeon Åke Senning. The device helps the heart keep the right rhythm and saves people with conduction disorders.","fact":"The first patient to receive an implanted pacemaker lived for several more decades, through many replacements of the device.","wiki_en":"Artificial cardiac pacemaker"},{"name":"Insulin","emoji":"🧪","meta":["1921","Banting and Best","medicine"],"description":"The hormone used to treat diabetes was isolated by the Canadian researchers Frederick Banting and Charles Best in a laboratory at the University of Toronto. Before that discovery a diagnosis of type 1 diabetes was very nearly a death sentence.","fact":"Its discoverers sold the insulin patent to the university for a symbolic single dollar, so the medicine would stay affordable.","wiki_en":"Insulin"},{"name":"Contact lens","emoji":"👁️","meta":["1887–1888","several inventors","medicine"],"description":"The first glass lenses laid straight onto the eye to correct vision were proposed independently by several European specialists, including the German glassmaker F. A. Müller. Comfortable soft polymer lenses came much later, in the mid-20th century, thanks to the Czech chemist Otto Wichterle.","fact":"Wichterle built the first soft contact lens prototype at home, on a homemade device that looked like a record player.","wiki_en":"Contact lens"},{"name":"Bank card","emoji":"💳","meta":["1950","Diners Club","finance"],"description":"The first widely used payment card, for settling restaurant bills, was issued by the American company Diners Club. It replaced cash for regular customers, and banks soon began issuing cards of their own, which started modern cashless payment.","fact":"The very first Diners Club card was made of stiff cardboard rather than plastic.","wiki_en":"Diners Club International"},{"name":"ATM","emoji":"🏧","meta":["1967","John Shepherd-Barron","finance"],"description":"The first cash machine that handed out money with no teller involved was installed in London by the inventor John Shepherd-Barron, who thought of the idea while taking a bath. It freed people from having to fit their lives around branch opening hours.","fact":"A commemorative plaque now marks the spot in Enfield, London, where the first cash machine went into service.","wiki_en":"Automated teller machine"},{"name":"Barcode","emoji":"🏷️","meta":["1952","Woodland and Silver","retail"],"description":"The idea of coding information about goods as a pattern of stripes came from the Americans Norman Woodland and Bernard Silver, inspired by Morse code, which they drew with their fingers in the sand. Barcodes only came into wide use two decades later, as scanners improved.","fact":"The first item ever scanned by barcode at a real supermarket till was a packet of chewing gum.","wiki_en":"Barcode"},{"name":"Bitcoin","emoji":"🪙","meta":["2008–2009","Satoshi Nakamoto","finance"],"description":"The first decentralised cryptocurrency was described in a paper and then launched as working software by a person or group hiding behind the pseudonym Satoshi Nakamoto. Who actually created Bitcoin is still unknown to this day.","fact":"The first block in the Bitcoin chain held a coded reference to a newspaper headline about the bailing out of banks during the financial crisis.","wiki_en":"Bitcoin"},{"name":"Neural network","emoji":"🧠","meta":["mid-20th century","McCulloch and Pitts","artificial intelligence"],"description":"A mathematical model imitating the way neurons in the brain work was first proposed by the scientists Warren McCulloch and Walter Pitts. Their ideas led to Frank Rosenblatt's perceptron and, decades later, to the deep neural networks behind today's artificial intelligence.","fact":"The first models of artificial neurons appeared in the 1940s, long before computers were powerful enough to train them.","wiki_en":"Artificial neural network"},{"name":"Robot","emoji":"🤖","meta":["20th century","several inventors","automation"],"description":"The first programmable industrial arms for automating factory work were built by engineers in the mid-20th century, among them the American George Devol with his Unimate. The word robot itself had been heard much earlier, in a play by the Czech writer Karel Čapek, who got it from his brother Josef.","fact":"Robot comes from a Czech word for hard, forced labour.","wiki_en":"Robot"},{"name":"Assembly line","emoji":"🏭","meta":["1913","Henry Ford","manufacturing"],"description":"The moving assembly line for mass-producing cars was introduced by the American industrialist Henry Ford at his own factory. It sharply sped up production and cut prices, putting a car within reach of ordinary people.","fact":"Ford's assembly line cut the time needed to build one car from more than twelve hours to about an hour and a half.","wiki_en":"Assembly line"},{"name":"Traffic light","emoji":"🚦","meta":["1868","John Peake Knight","road traffic"],"description":"The first device for directing street traffic with coloured signals was put up next to the British Parliament to a design by the engineer John Peake Knight. It ran on gas lamps and soon exploded, after which the idea was dropped until the age of the motor car.","fact":"London's first traffic light exploded just a few weeks after installation because of a gas leak.","wiki_en":"Traffic light"},{"name":"Chess","emoji":"♟️","meta":["around the 6th century","India","board games"],"description":"The game that became the ancestor of modern chess appeared in ancient India under the name chaturanga and depicted troops drawn up for battle. Through Persia and the Arab world it reached Europe, where it slowly acquired the rules and pieces we know.","fact":"The word checkmate comes from the Persian phrase shah mat, meaning that the king is defeated.","wiki_en":"Chess"},{"name":"Rubik's Cube","emoji":"🧩","meta":["1974","Ernő Rubik","puzzles"],"description":"The mechanical puzzle of coloured rotating faces was thought up by the Hungarian architect and teacher Ernő Rubik as a way of explaining movement in space to his students. The toy became one of the best-selling products in history and spawned a sport of its own, speedcubing.","fact":"By Rubik's own account, it took him almost a month to solve the cube he had invented for the very first time.","wiki_en":"Rubik's Cube"},{"name":"Use of fire","emoji":"🔥","meta":["prehistory","brought under control","survival and daily life"],"description":"Mastering fire was one of the great turning points in human history: it made it possible to cook food, to keep warm in the cold and to fend off predators. Exactly when people learned not merely to use fire but to keep and kindle it is still argued over.","fact":"Scientists still disagree on when early humans moved from using fire by chance to lighting it regularly.","wiki_en":"Control of fire by early humans"},{"name":"Plough","emoji":"🐂","meta":["the Neolithic","various farming cultures","agriculture"],"description":"A simple tool for breaking up soil before sowing changed farming completely, letting people work far larger fields than they ever could by hand. Early ploughs were pulled by animals, and over time the design grew more complex and more effective.","fact":"The arrival of the plough is considered one of the things that let ancient societies settle into permanent farming.","wiki_en":"Plough"},{"name":"Sail","emoji":"⛵","meta":["the ancient world","several civilisations independently","seafaring"],"description":"Using the force of the wind to move a vessel arose independently in several ancient civilisations, Egypt and Mesopotamia among them. The sail freed sailors from constant rowing and let them cover far greater distances than before.","fact":"Ancient Egyptian pictures of sailing boats on the Nile are among the earliest evidence of the sail in use.","wiki_en":"Sail"},{"name":"Paper clip","emoji":"📎","meta":["late 19th century","several inventors","stationery"],"description":"The simple device for holding sheets of paper together appeared in the late 19th century, when several inventors independently patented similar bent-wire shapes. The familiar loop took a while to settle in and gradually pushed the alternatives out.","fact":"Contrary to a common myth, the familiar shape of the paper clip was not invented by one particular person — the idea developed in parallel.","wiki_en":"Paper clip"},{"name":"Umbrella","emoji":"☂️","meta":["the ancient world","China, Egypt, the Middle East","protection from weather"],"description":"A device for keeping off sun and rain has been known since deep antiquity; similar things were used in ancient China, Egypt and Mesopotamia. The folding umbrella in its modern form only spread later, as technology and materials improved.","fact":"In some ancient cultures the umbrella was for a long time not a shield from the weather but a sign of power and high rank.","wiki_en":"Umbrella"},{"name":"Matches","emoji":"🔥","meta":["first half of the 19th century","several inventors in Europe","household chemistry"],"description":"An easy way of making a flame on demand spread in the first half of the 19th century, when chemists in several European countries independently worked out mixtures for match heads. Early versions were temperamental and at times dangerous to use.","fact":"Early chemical matches caught fire unpredictably and threw off sparks, which made them unsafe to handle.","wiki_en":"Match"},{"name":"Scissors","emoji":"✂️","meta":["the ancient world","Egypt and ancient Rome","daily life and craft"],"description":"A cutting tool made of two joined blades has been known since deep antiquity: simple spring shears were already used in ancient Egypt. The more familiar design, with the blades joined by a pivot, spread in Roman times.","fact":"Early scissors were not the pivoted tool we know but two blades joined by a flexible springy arc.","wiki_en":"Scissors"},{"name":"Button","emoji":"🔘","meta":["the Bronze Age","first ornament, later fastener","clothing"],"description":"Buttons began as decoration on clothing rather than as fasteners; they only started doing garments up much later, once buttonholes appeared. Simple ornamental buttons turn up in excavations of Bronze Age settlements.","fact":"By tradition buttons are sewn on different sides for men and women: on the right for men, on the left for women.","wiki_en":"Button"},{"name":"Lock","emoji":"🔒","meta":["the ancient world","Egypt and Mesopotamia","security"],"description":"Simple wooden contrivances for bolting doors were in use in ancient Egypt and Mesopotamia thousands of years before our era. Over time the mechanisms grew more intricate, and metal increasingly took the place of wood.","fact":"One of the oldest known locks is a wooden pin device found during excavations in the Middle East.","wiki_en":"Lock (security device)"},{"name":"Nail","emoji":"🛠️","meta":["the Bronze Age","Egypt and Mesopotamia","construction"],"description":"This simple metal fastener was already in use in the Bronze Age, once smiths learned to forge nails out of copper and bronze. Making them by machine came much later, which is what turned nails into something cheap and universal.","fact":"Before machine production every nail was forged by hand, which made them a relatively expensive item.","wiki_en":"Nail (fastener)"},{"name":"Corkscrew","emoji":"🍾","meta":["17th century","England","daily life"],"description":"A tool for pulling stoppers out of bottles appeared once glass bottles with tight cork stoppers became the usual way of keeping wine. The first corkscrews looked much like the tools used for cleaning out the barrels of firearms.","fact":"The shape of the corkscrew is thought to descend from a device for pulling wadding out of the barrels of old guns.","wiki_en":"Corkscrew"},{"name":"Coffee maker","emoji":"☕","meta":["19th century","Europe","household appliances"],"description":"Devices for making coffee developed through the 19th century, as inventors looked for ways to brew it faster and more conveniently than the traditional boiling in a pot on the stove. Since then countless designs have appeared, from drip machines to espresso makers.","fact":"The first prototypes of pressure coffee machines appeared in Europe as early as the 19th century, long before modern espresso machines.","wiki_en":"Coffeemaker"},{"name":"Toaster","emoji":"🍞","meta":["early 20th century","USA","household appliances"],"description":"The electric appliance for browning bread appeared in the early 20th century, along with electricity in ordinary homes. The model we know today, which switches off by itself and pops the toast up, came a little later.","fact":"The first electric toasters had no automatic cut-off — you had to watch the bread and turn it by hand.","wiki_en":"Toaster"},{"name":"Hair dryer","emoji":"💨","meta":["late 19th century","Europe","household appliances"],"description":"The electric appliance for drying hair appeared at the turn of the 19th and 20th centuries, when hairdressers began swapping bulky fixed machines for compact handheld ones. In time the hair dryer became one of the most common appliances in the house.","fact":"The first hair dryers were so heavy and unwieldy that they were used only in professional salons.","wiki_en":"Hair dryer"},{"name":"Aerosol spray can","emoji":"🧴","meta":["first half of the 20th century","Norway and the USA","household chemistry"],"description":"The principle of spraying liquid under gas pressure was worked out in the first half of the 20th century, and aerosol cans came into wide use after the Second World War. Today they hold everything from perfume to household cleaners.","fact":"One of the first mass uses of aerosol cans was for products to keep insects away.","wiki_en":"Aerosol spray"},{"name":"Dishwasher","emoji":"🍽️","meta":["late 19th century","USA","household appliances"],"description":"The first practical dishwasher, worked by hand, was built in the late 19th century in the hope of sparing people the tedium of washing up at the sink. It only became a common household appliance decades later, as electricity and plumbing spread.","fact":"One of the first practical dishwashers is credited to an American woman who was tired of servants chipping her china.","wiki_en":"Dishwasher"},{"name":"Seat belt","emoji":"🛡️","meta":["19th–20th centuries","several inventors","car safety"],"description":"Simple straps for holding a passenger in place in aircraft and cars were proposed as early as the 19th century, but the three-point belt we know was patented much later, in the mid-20th. In time the seat belt became compulsory in every car.","fact":"The inventor of the modern three-point belt made the patent freely available so that every carmaker could use it.","wiki_en":"Seat belt"},{"name":"Airbag","emoji":"🚗","meta":["mid-20th century","several inventors","car safety"],"description":"The idea of a cushion that inflates in an instant to soften a crash was proposed independently by several inventors in the middle of the 20th century. Manufacturers only began fitting airbags to production cars as standard decades later.","fact":"The first patents for inflatable devices to cushion impacts date from the 1950s, decades before airbags went into cars in quantity.","wiki_en":"Airbag"},{"name":"Pneumatic tyre","emoji":"🛞","meta":["19th century","Great Britain","transport"],"description":"The air-filled rubber tyre, which smooths a vehicle's ride enormously, was patented in the 19th century and first came into wide use on bicycles. The technology later moved to cars and became the standard for everything on wheels.","fact":"The pneumatic tyre was first tried on bicycles rather than cars, which barely existed at the time.","wiki_en":"Tire"},{"name":"Lithium-ion battery","emoji":"🔋","meta":["late 20th century","several laboratories","electronics and transport"],"description":"The rechargeable lithium battery was developed by several research groups over the closing decades of the 20th century and went into wide commercial use in the 1990s. Such cells now power smartphones, laptops and electric cars.","fact":"Developing the lithium-ion battery took several decades and drew together the work of scientists in different countries.","wiki_en":"Lithium-ion battery"},{"name":"Jet engine","emoji":"✈️","meta":["1930s","Great Britain and Germany","aviation"],"description":"The principle of jet propulsion for aircraft was developed independently by engineers in Britain and in Germany during the 1930s. Jet engines let aeroplanes fly far faster and higher than machines with piston motors ever could.","fact":"British and German engineers reached the jet engine independently, but the first aircraft into the air had a German engine.","wiki_en":"Jet engine"},{"name":"QR code","emoji":"🔲","meta":["1994","Japan","technology and labelling"],"description":"The two-dimensional barcode, able to hold far more information than an ordinary one, was developed in Japan for the needs of the car industry. Years later QR codes turned up everywhere, from paying for shopping to links to websites.","fact":"QR codes were created to track car parts on the assembly line, not for smartphones, which did not yet exist.","wiki_en":"QR code"},{"name":"E-reader","emoji":"📖","meta":["late 20th century","several companies","reading and technology"],"description":"The idea of a device for reading texts in electronic form was discussed back in the 20th century, but genuinely comfortable models only arrived with screens based on electronic ink. Such devices made it far easier to store and carry whole libraries.","fact":"Many e-reader screens use electronic ink, which draws almost no power while a still page is on display.","wiki_en":"E-reader"},{"name":"MP3 player","emoji":"🎧","meta":["1990s","Germany and South Korea","portable electronics"],"description":"The MP3 sound compression format was developed by German engineers at the end of the 20th century, and portable devices for playing it followed soon after. MP3 players let people carry thousands of songs instead of a handful of tapes or discs.","fact":"The name MP3 comes from the international standard for compressing video and sound of which the format was originally a part.","wiki_en":"Portable media player"},{"name":"Headphones","emoji":"🎧","meta":["late 19th century","USA","audio equipment"],"description":"The first devices for listening to sound privately appeared in the late 19th century along with telephony, though the light, compact shape we know settled in much later. Headphones now come in dozens of designs, from over-ear to wireless.","fact":"Early headphones were heavy and unwieldy, so they were used mostly by telephone and radio operators rather than by music listeners.","wiki_en":"Headphones"},{"name":"Video games","emoji":"🕹️","meta":["mid-20th century","USA","entertainment and technology"],"description":"The first crude computer games were written by scientists as far back as the middle of the 20th century, as experiments and demonstrations of what computing machines could do. Over time video games grew into one of the largest entertainment industries in the world.","fact":"One of the first known computer games was made by a scientist as a way of showing visitors what a computing machine could do.","wiki_en":"Video game"},{"name":"Games console","emoji":"🎮","meta":["1970s","USA","entertainment"],"description":"A device for playing games that plugged into an ordinary television appeared at the turn of the 1960s and 1970s and brought video games into the home. Several generations of consoles have followed since, each with more graphical power than the last.","fact":"The earliest consoles offered only a few simple built-in games, with no way of changing them.","wiki_en":"Video game console"},{"name":"Virtual reality","emoji":"🥽","meta":["second half of the 20th century","several researchers","technology"],"description":"The idea of immersing a user completely in a computer-generated space was worked on by researchers over several decades in the second half of the 20th century. Early headsets were bulky, and the technology only reached a mass audience in the 21st century.","fact":"The first experimental virtual reality headsets were so heavy that they had to be suspended from the ceiling.","wiki_en":"Virtual reality"},{"name":"Augmented reality","emoji":"📱","meta":["late 20th century","several researchers","technology"],"description":"The technology of laying digital images over the real world began to develop in research laboratories at the end of the 20th century. Augmented reality is used today in smartphones, in games and in industrial applications.","fact":"One of the first terms for augmented reality appeared among researchers as early as the beginning of the 1990s.","wiki_en":"Augmented reality"},{"name":"Social media","emoji":"📱","meta":["turn of the 20th and 21st centuries","several companies","online communication"],"description":"The first sites that let you create a profile and add other users as friends appeared around the turn of the 20th and 21st centuries. Social networks have since become one of the main ways people talk, share news and promote a business.","fact":"One of the first genuinely mass social networks appeared before smartphones were at all widespread.","wiki_en":"Social media"},{"name":"Search engine","emoji":"🔍","meta":["1990s","several companies","the internet"],"description":"The first systems for finding information on the internet appeared in the early 1990s, when the number of sites grew too large to catalogue by hand. Over time search algorithms became more and more complex, and the results more accurate.","fact":"Early search engines were often just hand-compiled directories of sites rather than automatic analysis of pages.","wiki_en":"Web search engine"},{"name":"Instant messenger","emoji":"💬","meta":["turn of the 20th and 21st centuries","several companies","online communication"],"description":"Programs for exchanging text messages quickly over the internet came into wide use around the turn of the 20th and 21st centuries. Modern messengers long ago outgrew plain text, adding calls, file sharing and group chats.","fact":"Early messengers ran only on personal computers; mobile versions appeared much later.","wiki_en":"Instant messaging"},{"name":"Cryptocurrency","emoji":"🪙","meta":["2008–2009","an author under a pseudonym","finance and technology"],"description":"The first cryptocurrency appeared at the end of the 2000s, following the publication of a technical paper by an author hiding behind a pseudonym. The distributed ledger technology behind it later became the basis for a great many other digital currencies.","fact":"The real identity of the author of the first cryptocurrency is still unknown, despite many attempts to establish it.","wiki_en":"Cryptocurrency","wiki_ru":"Криптовалюта"},{"name":"Artificial intelligence","emoji":"🤖","meta":["mid-20th century","several scientists","science and technology"],"description":"The term and the field devoted to building systems that can handle work requiring human intelligence took shape in the middle of the 20th century, where mathematics met computer science. Over the decades the field has been through several waves of enthusiasm and disappointment.","fact":"The term artificial intelligence itself was proposed at an academic conference in the mid-1950s.","wiki_en":"Artificial intelligence","wiki_ru":"Искусственный интеллект (фильм)"},{"name":"Genetic engineering","emoji":"🧬","meta":["1970s","several scientists","biotechnology"],"description":"Methods for deliberately altering the genetic material of living things began to be developed in the 1970s, once scientists learned to move individual genes between different species. The technology underpins modern biotechnology and medicine.","fact":"The first experiments in moving genes between species set off fierce scientific and public debate as early as the 1970s.","wiki_en":"Genetic engineering"},{"name":"Cloning","emoji":"🐑","meta":["1990s","Great Britain","biotechnology"],"description":"The technology for producing a genetic copy of a living organism became widely known to the public after a mammal was successfully cloned at the end of the 20th century. The method is now used mainly in scientific and agricultural research.","fact":"The best-known case of cloning was a sheep born in the late 1990s, who became a worldwide sensation.","wiki_en":"Cloning"},{"name":"Discovery of the structure of DNA","emoji":"🧬","meta":["1953","several scientists","biology"],"description":"The double helix model of DNA, which describes how the molecule of heredity is built, was proposed in the middle of the 20th century by a group of scientists who drew, among other things, on colleagues' X-ray diffraction data. It was one of the key milestones in biology.","fact":"X-ray images taken by another researcher, whose contribution was long undervalued, played a vital part in working out the structure of DNA.","wiki_en":"DNA"},{"name":"Periodic table","emoji":"⚗️","meta":["1869","Russia","chemistry"],"description":"A system ordering the chemical elements by their properties and their atomic mass was proposed by a Russian chemist in the second half of the 19th century. The table proved so accurate that it predicted the existence of elements nobody had yet discovered.","fact":"Its author managed to predict the properties of several elements that had not been discovered when he published.","wiki_en":"Periodic table"},{"name":"Theory of relativity","emoji":"🌌","meta":["early 20th century","Albert Einstein","physics"],"description":"The physical theory that changed our picture of space, time and gravity was created by Albert Einstein at the beginning of the 20th century. It explained a number of phenomena classical physics could not, and became the basis of modern cosmology.","fact":"One prediction of relativity, the bending of light near massive objects, was confirmed by observing a solar eclipse.","wiki_en":"Theory of relativity"},{"name":"Theory of evolution","emoji":"🐒","meta":["1859","Great Britain","biology"],"description":"The idea that species change gradually under the pressure of natural selection was set out in detail by a British naturalist in the middle of the 19th century, after years of observation and travel. The theory became the foundation of modern biology.","fact":"The author gathered the key observations for his future theory during a long voyage around the world in his youth.","wiki_en":"Evolution"},{"name":"Newton's law of universal gravitation","emoji":"🍎","meta":["late 17th century","Great Britain","physics"],"description":"The law describing the force of attraction between bodies anywhere in the universe was formulated by an English scientist at the end of the 17th century. It became one of the foundations of classical mechanics and explained the motion of planets, the tides and falling objects.","fact":"According to legend, the idea of universal gravitation came to the scientist after he watched an apple fall.","wiki_en":"Newton's law of universal gravitation","wiki_ru":"Классическая теория тяготения Ньютона"},{"name":"Electricity","emoji":"⚡","meta":["18th–19th centuries","several scientists","physics"],"description":"An understanding of what electricity actually is took shape gradually through the 18th and 19th centuries, through the work of many scientists in many countries. From the first experiments with static charge, science travelled a long road to generators and power grids.","fact":"The first public experiments with electricity were taken by audiences more as a spectacular entertainment than as serious science.","wiki_en":"Electricity"},{"name":"Lightning rod","emoji":"⚡","meta":["18th century","USA","building safety"],"description":"A device for protecting buildings from lightning strikes was proposed in the 18th century by an American inventor and scientist who studied the nature of atmospheric electricity. A simple metal construction carried the discharge into the ground, bypassing the building.","fact":"The inventor of the lightning rod is also famous for his celebrated, if dangerous, experiment with a kite in a thunderstorm.","wiki_en":"Lightning rod"},{"name":"Thermometer","emoji":"🌡️","meta":["16th–17th centuries","several inventors","measuring temperature"],"description":"The first instruments for measuring temperature were built in Europe at the turn of the 16th and 17th centuries, and the precisely graduated scales we use came somewhat later. Thermometers have since gone from bulky glass tubes to compact digital devices.","fact":"Different scientists proposed temperature scales of their own, so for a long time several incompatible systems were in use at once.","wiki_en":"Thermometer"},{"name":"Barometer","emoji":"🌦️","meta":["17th century","Italy","measuring pressure"],"description":"The instrument for measuring atmospheric pressure was built by an Italian scientist in the middle of the 17th century, working with a tube of mercury. The discovery showed that air has weight and pressure, an important step towards the meteorology to come.","fact":"The inventor of the barometer was a pupil of the famous Italian astronomer and carried on his experiments with pressure and vacuum.","wiki_en":"Barometer"},{"name":"Kick scooter","emoji":"🛴","meta":["early 20th century","several inventors","transport"],"description":"A simple way of getting about, with handlebars and a footboard, driven along by pushing off the ground with one foot. Homemade scooters turned up in cities as early as the 1900s, and the thing was long thought of as a child's toy before it became real urban transport.","fact":"Adults took an interest in scooters again thanks to the light folding aluminium models that appeared in the late 1990s.","wiki_en":"Kick scooter"},{"name":"Electric scooter","emoji":"🛴","meta":["early 21st century","several companies","urban transport"],"description":"The compact electric vehicle built on the classic kick scooter came into mass use in the late 2010s, along with the spread of rental services. Electric scooters are now a visible part of city mobility in many countries around the world.","fact":"Many cities have had to write separate rules for electric scooters, from speed limits to bans on riding along pavements.","wiki_en":"Motorized scooter"},{"name":"Electron microscope","emoji":"🔬","meta":["1930s","Germany","science"],"description":"The instrument that uses a beam of electrons instead of light to produce a magnified image was developed by German engineers in the 1930s. The electron microscope let scientists see, for the first time, objects too small for any optical device.","fact":"The resolving power of an electron microscope is thousands of times greater than that of a classical light microscope.","wiki_en":"Electron microscope"},{"name":"Large Hadron Collider","emoji":"⚛️","meta":["2008","CERN (Europe)","physics"],"description":"The largest particle accelerator in the world was built on the border of France and Switzerland by an international scientific organisation. With it, physicists confirmed the existence of the Higgs boson, a particle that had long been only a theoretical prediction.","fact":"The collider's ring tunnel runs underground and is several dozen kilometres around.","wiki_en":"Large Hadron Collider"},{"name":"Discovery of blood groups","emoji":"🩸","meta":["1900","Austria","medicine"],"description":"The system of human blood groups was discovered by an Austrian scientist at the turn of the 19th and 20th centuries, when he found that mixing blood from different people sometimes made the red cells clump together. The discovery made safe transfusion possible and saved millions of lives.","fact":"Before blood groups were known, transfusions often ended tragically precisely because donor and patient were incompatible.","wiki_en":"Blood type"},{"name":"Antibiotics","emoji":"💊","meta":["1928","Great Britain","medicine"],"description":"The first antibiotic was found by accident by a British scientist who noticed that mould was holding back the growth of bacteria in a culture dish. The discovery revolutionised medicine, turning many once-fatal infections into curable illnesses.","fact":"As the story goes, the crucial discovery was almost an accident: the scientist had simply left a dish of bacteria out on his bench.","wiki_en":"Antibiotic"},{"name":"Syringe","emoji":"💉","meta":["19th century","France and Scotland","medicine"],"description":"The device for injecting liquids under the skin with a hollow needle and a plunger was developed in the middle of the 19th century by several doctors in Europe working independently of one another. The invention made it possible to dose medicines and vaccines precisely.","fact":"Early syringes were made of glass and metal; disposable plastic ones appeared only more than a century later.","wiki_en":"Syringe"},{"name":"Stethoscope","emoji":"🩺","meta":["1816","France","medicine"],"description":"The instrument for listening to sounds inside the body was invented by a French doctor at the beginning of the 19th century, who rolled a sheet of paper into a tube rather than press his ear directly against a female patient. That simple solution founded a whole branch of diagnosis.","fact":"The first stethoscope was an ordinary paper tube, not the device with tubing and earpieces we know today.","wiki_en":"Stethoscope"},{"name":"Hearing aid","emoji":"👂","meta":["19th–20th centuries","several inventors","medicine"],"description":"Devices to amplify sound for people with impaired hearing developed through the 19th and 20th centuries, from simple ear trumpets to electronic instruments. As electronics advanced, the aids became steadily smaller and more powerful.","fact":"The first hearing aids were plain trumpets without a single electronic part, amplifying sound purely mechanically.","wiki_en":"Hearing aid"},{"name":"Prosthesis","emoji":"🦿","meta":["the ancient world","various civilisations","medicine"],"description":"People were making artificial replacements for lost body parts in deep antiquity: simple wooden and metal prostheses turn up in excavations of ancient settlements. Modern versions use light materials and electronics, and greatly widen what their users can do.","fact":"One of the oldest known prostheses is a wooden toe found during excavations in ancient Egypt.","wiki_en":"Prosthesis"},{"name":"Artificial heart","emoji":"🫀","meta":["1980s","USA","medicine"],"description":"A mechanical device able to replace the work of the heart temporarily or in part began to be implanted successfully in patients in the 1980s. The technology became an important way of keeping people alive while they wait for a donor heart.","fact":"The first fully artificial hearts kept patients alive only a few months, but they paved the way for today's models.","wiki_en":"Artificial heart"},{"name":"Powered exoskeleton","emoji":"🦾","meta":["second half of the 20th century","several countries","medicine and robotics"],"description":"Mechanical frames worn on the body to boost muscle power or help with walking have been developed since the middle of the 20th century by military and medical researchers. Exoskeletons are used today both in rehabilitation and on the factory floor.","fact":"Some of the first experimental exoskeletons were so heavy and bulky that they themselves got in the way of free movement.","wiki_en":"Powered exoskeleton"},{"name":"Typewriter","emoji":"⌨️","meta":["19th century","several inventors","office work"],"description":"The mechanical device that printed text by striking type through an inked ribbon was worked on by several inventors through the 19th century. For decades the typewriter was the main tool of office work, until computers pushed it aside.","fact":"The arrangement of letters devised for mechanical typewriters is still used on computer keyboards today.","wiki_en":"Typewriter"},{"name":"Abacus","emoji":"🧮","meta":["the ancient world","the Middle East and Asia","counting and trade"],"description":"The simple counting device with beads that slide along rods was used by merchants and clerks in many ancient civilisations, from Mesopotamia to China. In some countries the abacus stayed on a bookkeeper's desk even in the age of the calculator.","fact":"In some countries skilled abacus users could calculate almost as fast as the early electronic calculators.","wiki_en":"Abacus"},{"name":"Calculator","emoji":"🔢","meta":["17th century, mechanical","20th century, electronic","calculation"],"description":"Mechanical devices for doing arithmetic were built as early as the 17th century, while compact electronic calculators only appeared in the second half of the 20th. Their spread made everyday sums far easier in science, in trade and at home.","fact":"The first mechanical arithmetic machines were so complex and expensive that they remained curiosities rather than working tools.","wiki_en":"Calculator"},{"name":"Electric motor","emoji":"🔌","meta":["1830s","several scientists","electrical engineering"],"description":"The device that turns electrical energy into mechanical movement was developed by several scientists in different countries during the 1830s. Electric motors went on to underpin a vast range of technology, from factory machines to kitchen appliances.","fact":"Early electric motors were laboratory demonstrations rather than practical devices for real work.","wiki_en":"Electric motor"},{"name":"Transformer","emoji":"🔌","meta":["1880s","several engineers","electrical engineering"],"description":"The device for changing the voltage of alternating current was developed by engineers in the 1880s, which is what made it possible to carry electricity over long distances. Transformers remain a key part of every power system today.","fact":"Without transformers, carrying electricity over long distances would make no economic sense because of the enormous losses.","wiki_en":"Transformer"},{"name":"Iron","emoji":"♨️","meta":["antiquity","electric from the 19th century","daily life"],"description":"Smoothing cloth with a heated object was already done in ancient China, using metal pans filled with hot coals. The electric iron we know today appeared much later, at the end of the 19th century, once homes had electricity.","fact":"Before electricity, irons were heated over an open flame or filled with glowing coals inside the body of the iron itself.","wiki_en":"Clothes iron"},{"name":"Multicooker","emoji":"🍲","meta":["turn of the 20th and 21st centuries","several countries","household appliances"],"description":"The electric appliance that combines the jobs of several kitchen devices, cooking slowly and in many modes, came into wide use in the 2000s. A multicooker will prepare a dish with hardly any attention from the cook.","fact":"Many multicooker models can not only boil and stew but also bake, which widens their use in the kitchen considerably.","wiki_en":"Multicooker"},{"name":"Can opener","emoji":"🥫","meta":["mid-19th century","Great Britain and the USA","daily life"],"description":"A special knife for opening tins appeared decades after the tin itself — at first cans were opened with a hammer and chisel. More convenient and safer can openers arrived in the second half of the 19th century.","fact":"The first tin cans were so sturdy that the label advised opening them with a hammer and chisel.","wiki_en":"Can opener"},{"name":"Fax","emoji":"📠","meta":["the idea in the 19th century","widespread in the 20th","communications and office work"],"description":"The idea of sending images along electrical wires appeared as early as the middle of the 19th century, long before the telephone was invented. The fax machine only became a genuine office staple, though, in the second half of the 20th century.","fact":"The first patent for sending images along wires went to the Scottish inventor Alexander Bain back in the 1840s.","wiki_en":"Fax"},{"name":"Photocopier","emoji":"🖨️","meta":["mid-20th century","USA","office work"],"description":"The technique of copying documents dry, with no liquid chemicals, was developed by an American inventor in the middle of the 20th century. The invention founded a whole office equipment industry and changed how the world handles paperwork.","fact":"The first prototypes of the photocopier failed for years to interest any large company before the technology caught on.","wiki_en":"Photocopier"},{"name":"Portable cassette player","emoji":"📼","meta":["1979","Japan","portable audio"],"description":"The compact portable device for listening to music on cassettes was released by a Japanese company at the end of the 1970s and changed the habits of millions of listeners. It made music genuinely mobile for the first time.","fact":"The first model had two headphone sockets: the assumption was that people would listen to music in pairs.","wiki_en":"Walkman"},{"name":"Vinyl record","emoji":"📀","meta":["mid-20th century","USA","sound recording"],"description":"The long-playing vinyl record, turning at a fixed speed, replaced the more fragile shellac discs, which held far less music, in the middle of the 20th century. Digital formats notwithstanding, vinyl is still loved by collectors and music fans today.","fact":"Decades into the digital era, sales of vinyl records rose sharply again among music lovers.","wiki_en":"Phonograph record"},{"name":"Fire extinguisher","emoji":"🧯","meta":["18th–19th centuries","Great Britain","fire safety"],"description":"The first portable devices for putting out fires with chemicals appeared in the 18th and 19th centuries, gradually replacing buckets of water and sand. The design changed over time, and the mixtures inside became more effective and safer.","fact":"Early extinguishers were often just glass vessels full of solution, which you simply threw into the flames.","wiki_en":"Fire extinguisher"},{"name":"Loudspeaker","emoji":"🔊","meta":["early 20th century","several inventors","audio equipment"],"description":"The device for amplifying and reproducing sound for a large audience was developed in the early 20th century, alongside electrical engineering and radio. Loudspeakers made public speeches, concerts and broadcasts to big halls and squares possible.","fact":"The first loudspeakers were far behind modern ones in sound quality, but they could already reach thousands of listeners at once.","wiki_en":"Loudspeaker"},{"name":"Microphone","emoji":"🎤","meta":["late 19th century","several inventors","audio and communications"],"description":"The device that turns sound into an electrical signal was developed by several inventors at the end of the 19th century, in parallel with telephony. The microphone became the basis not only of the telephone but of recording, radio and live performance.","fact":"Early microphones were built first of all for telephony, not for music or public speaking.","wiki_en":"Microphone"},{"name":"Synthesizer","emoji":"🎹","meta":["mid-20th century","USA","music"],"description":"The electronic musical instrument able to produce and shape sounds with electrical circuits began to develop in the middle of the 20th century. Synthesizers vastly widened the palette of sounds available to musicians and gave rise to whole genres.","fact":"Early synthesizers filled entire rooms and took real technical skill to operate.","wiki_en":"Synthesizer"},{"name":"Electric guitar","emoji":"🎸","meta":["1930s","USA","music"],"description":"Guitars with electromagnetic pickups, amplified through a speaker, went into production in the 1930s so that the instrument could be heard in a large ensemble. The electric guitar became one of the great symbols of 20th-century rock music.","fact":"One of the main reasons the electric guitar appeared was the need to cut through the brass in big bands.","wiki_en":"Electric guitar","wiki_ru":"Электрогитара"},{"name":"Piano","emoji":"🎹","meta":["early 18th century","Italy","music"],"description":"The keyboard instrument whose sound comes from hammers striking strings was created by an Italian craftsman at the beginning of the 18th century. Its great advantage was that it could play softly or loudly depending on how hard the keys were struck.","fact":"The instrument's name comes from the Italian words for soft and loud — exactly the novelty that set it apart from its predecessors.","wiki_en":"Piano"},{"name":"Fireworks","emoji":"🎆","meta":["ancient China","China","festive culture"],"description":"Pyrotechnic mixtures based on gunpowder, used to throw bright fire into the sky, were first set off in ancient China many centuries ago, originally to frighten away evil spirits. Fireworks later spread worldwide and became part of every celebration.","fact":"Legend has it that the idea came from watching bamboo that had fallen into a fire crack loudly as it heated.","wiki_en":"Fireworks"},{"name":"Kite","emoji":"🪁","meta":["ancient China","China","leisure and science"],"description":"The light construction of frame and fabric, held in the air on a string, was invented in ancient China several thousand years ago. In time kites came to be used not only for fun but in scientific experiments as well.","fact":"Kites were used in research too — in the famous experiment on atmospheric electricity during a thunderstorm, for instance.","wiki_en":"Kite"},{"name":"Stopwatch","emoji":"⏱️","meta":["18th–19th centuries","several watchmakers","measuring time"],"description":"A clock mechanism able to time short intervals precisely was developed by watchmakers over the 18th and 19th centuries. The stopwatch became indispensable in sport, in science and in industry, wherever exact timing matters.","fact":"Early stopwatches were mechanical and had to be wound by hand, unlike today's electronic models.","wiki_en":"Stopwatch"},{"name":"Metronome","emoji":"🎵","meta":["early 19th century","Germany and the Netherlands","music"],"description":"The mechanical device that beats out an even rhythm at a chosen speed was patented by a German inventor at the beginning of the 19th century, building on an earlier Dutch design. The metronome became an important tool for musicians working on a steady tempo.","fact":"One well-known composer of the early 19th century was among the first to write exact metronome tempos into his scores.","wiki_en":"Metronome"},{"name":"Robot vacuum cleaner","emoji":"🤖","meta":["turn of the 20th and 21st centuries","several companies","household appliances"],"description":"The automatic floor cleaner that moves around a room on its own and steers around obstacles reached the market at the turn of the 20th and 21st centuries. As sensors and navigation algorithms improved, such machines began to clean more thoroughly and precisely.","fact":"Modern robot vacuums can build a map of the home and remember it for the next clean.","wiki_en":"Robotic vacuum cleaner"}];
